\fs96 \cf2 Compile-time evaluation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To understand Mojo's metaprogramming, you need to understand how Mojo runs code at compile time. Several things can trigger compile-time code execution:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Assigning an expression to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Evaluating a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conditional or loop.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Assigning an expression to a compile-time parameter.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
And a few less common cases, all identified with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here are some examples:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  SIZE \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 1024\cf14  \cf5 //\cf14  \cf15 32\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here the expression 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 1024 // 32
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  invokes the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 IntLiteral.__floordiv__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method. Since it occurs in a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  assignment, the method must be run at compile time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf18 range\cf5 (\cf15 4\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13    \cf5 print(\cf14 i\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 range(4)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method needs to run to produce an iterator for the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  array \cf5 =\cf14  InlineArray\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ,\cf14  get_array_size\cf5 ()]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 get_array_size()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function needs to run at compile time to determine the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 size
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter, which forms part of the type of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 array
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . (For example, if 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 get_array_size()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  returns 32, the type of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 array
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable is 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 InlineArray[Int, 32]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .)\
When the compiler encounters a function call in a compile-time context, the compiler runs the function separately, as if it was a small separate program. This is similar in concept to how C++ evaluates a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 constexpr
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . (For a slightly deeper look at this process, see \cf5 \ul \ulc5 How the compiler runs code\cf2 \ulnone .)\
While most code can run at compile time, Mojo won't run code that depends on the execution environment. The following are examples of code that Mojo won't run at compile time:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
File I/O.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Foreign function calls (for example, to external libraries).\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Functions that can {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#raising-and-non-raising-functions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul raise errors}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In addition, the compiler can't run functions on the GPU. Compile-time functions in GPU code are actually run on the CPU.\
When running code, the compiler can allocate memory and instantiate types that allocate memory, such as strings and collections. With some limitations, it can pass compile-time values on to run-time code, a process called materialization. For more information, see the section on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/materialization/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul materialization}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 It is very common to want to name compile-time values. Whereas 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  defines a runtime value, we need a way to define a named compile-time constant. For this, Mojo uses a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  declaration. At its simplest, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can be used to define a constant value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  rows \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 512\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value is always evaluated at compile time, so you can use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to force a function to run at compile time. You can use this to calculate constant values based on information available at compile time, such as hardware parameters.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  block_size \cf5 =\cf14  _calculate_block_size\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Types are another common use for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values. Because types are compile-time expressions, you can use a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value as a shorthand (a type alias or "typedef") for a parameterized type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  Float16 \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 float16\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 1\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  UInt8 \cf5 =\cf14  SIMD\cf5 [\cf14 DType\cf5 .\cf14 uint8\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 1\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  x\cf5 :\cf14  Float16 \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 0\cf14   \cf6 # Float16 works like a "typedef"\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 (These aliases and others are actually defined in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/simd/#comptime-values"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul simd
\fs32 \cb1  module}}.)\
You can also parameterize a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value to express more complicated relationships. For details, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/#parameterized-comptime-values"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Parameterized 
\fs28 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cb1  values}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Compile-time scope\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variables, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values obey scope, and you can use local 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values within functions as you'd expect. Unlike 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variables, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values can be defined at the module level, outside of any function.\
The following constructs create a new compile-time scope:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Functions. The body of a function creates a new compile-time scope.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Compile-time flow control. Each branch of a compile-time conditional creates its own scope. The body of a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop also creates its own scope.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can only assign a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value to a given identifier once in a given scope.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  VALUE \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 10\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf18 scope_me\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 VALUE\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # prints 10\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  VALUE \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 20\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # comptime VALUE = 30  # error: invalid redeclaration of VALUE\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  \cf15 True\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 comptime\cf14  VALUE \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 40\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 VALUE\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # prints 40\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 VALUE\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # prints 20\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Compile-time flow control\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 One of the simplest things you can do with metaprogramming is using compile-time flow control to conditionalize or repeat code. Some sample uses include:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Conditionalizing platform-specific code (CPU vs. GPU, Linux vs. macOS) without runtime overhead.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Unrolling loops to eliminate runtime branches.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Handling different data types in generic code.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Unlike run-time flow control constructs, compile-time flow control constructs are evaluated once, at compile time, and determine what code is actually compiled.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Compile-time conditionals\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword to any 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  condition that's based on a valid compile-time expression (an expression that can be evaluated at compile time). This ensures that only the live branch of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement is compiled into the program, which can reduce your final binary size. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 sys \cf5 import\cf14  has_accelerator\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf18 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  has_accelerator\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         run_on_gpu\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 else:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         run_on_cpu\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this example, if no accelerator is available, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 run_on_gpu()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function is never called, or even compiled.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement can include 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 elif
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 else
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  branches just like a standard 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Compile-time loop unrolling\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop to create a loop that's fully unrolled at compile time. You should generally use this only for loops with small loop bodies and low iteration counts.\
The loop sequence must be a valid compile-time expression (that is, an expression that can be evaluated at compile time). For example, if you use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for i in range(LIMIT)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the expression 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 range(LIMIT)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  defines the loop sequence. This is a valid compile-time expression if 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LIMIT
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a parameter, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value, or integer literal.\
The compiler fully unrolls the loop by replacing the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LIMIT
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  copies of the loop body. The induction variable is replaced with a compile-time constant value for each "iteration." For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf18 range\cf5 (\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 5\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     b\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 -\cf15 1\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  a\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 +\cf14  a\cf5 [\cf14 i\cf5 -\cf15 1\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This is effectively unrolled to the following run-time code:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 b\cf5 [\cf15 0\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  a\cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 +\cf14  a\cf5 [\cf15 0\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 b\cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  a\cf5 [\cf15 2\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 +\cf14  a\cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 b\cf5 [\cf15 2\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  a\cf5 [\cf15 3\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 +\cf14  a\cf5 [\cf15 2\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 b\cf5 [\cf15 3\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  a\cf5 [\cf15 4\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 +\cf14  a\cf5 [\cf15 3\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This unrolled loop compiles to branchless machine code, unlike a normal 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop, which includes a bounds test at every iteration. This can be especially important on GPU, to avoid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/gpu/block-and-warp/#warp-level-synchronization"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul thread divergence}}.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  construct unrolls at the beginning of compilation, which can greatly expand both the code size and the compilation time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 How the compiler runs code\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The process of evaluating compile-time code involves three components of the compiler:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Parser. Parses the code into an intermediate representation (IR) and performs type checking.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Interpreter. Runs code at compile time.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Elaborator. Substitutes concrete values for compile-time parameters and produces concrete versions of parameterized functions and structs.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When the parser turns code into IR, it also replaces some very simple 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  expressions with their values, a process called constant folding. For example, the compiler can constant fold the expression 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 2 + 3
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 5
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Standard library functions that are marked 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @always_inline("builtin")
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are constant foldable. Compile-time expressions that can't be constant folded persist and are evaluated in the elaborator.\
When the elaborator encounters a function call in a compile-time context, it invokes the interpreter to run the function. The interpreter then checks whether the function being called has already been elaborated to produce a concrete, executable function. If not, the interpreter adds that function to the elaborator's work queue, and waits until it's done. Finally, the interpreter runs the concrete function\'97almost like it was a small separate program\'97and passes the return value back to the elaborator, which integrates it into the parsed IR.\
When reading code, it's important to remember that when a function is being interpreted at compile time, the function has been concretized: compile-time conditionals have been processed, and compile-time constraints and assertions have been tested. This sometimes appears to contradict the expectation that your code runs in the order it appears in the function. For example, if your function includes a compile-time assertion that fails, compilation fails before the interpreter enters the function, so no part of the function is evaluated\'97even code that occurs before the assertion.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri960\partightenfactor0
